v115  O(1)-CIRCUIT REALISATION OF THE OPTIMAL CROSS-PAULI ESTIMATOR
====================================================================================================
  N=3, M=9, Lambda=||H||=0.830259, grid t0=0.567581 (threshold pi/6L=0.630645)

====================================================================================================
CHECK 0  is half-range sampling valid, or does the kernel need time reversal?
====================================================================================================
  L is ODD. Half-range tau>=0 works only if the cross-Pauli correlation F
  is ODD too, making L*F even. Otherwise negative times are required.

      t        F(t)         F(-t)      F(t)+F(-t)     odd?
   --------------------------------------------------------------
    0.20   +0.381820   +0.419634   +0.801455
    0.50   +0.358478   +0.450834   +0.809312
    1.00   +0.335620   +0.504365   +0.839985

   F(0) = +0.400000   (an ODD function would have F(0)=0)

      quantity                         value
   ------------------------------------------------------------
   F'(0) central difference        -0.09494611
   full-range  int_R  L F dt       -0.09494622
   half-range  2 int_0^inf L F dt  +0.54734074

   HALF-RANGE INVALID AS twirl_cal IS BUILT - but the reason is a fixable
   readout choice, not the kernel and not the register.

   F(0) = +0.4000, i.e. the correlator carries a large EVEN part. An odd
   kernel annihilates that only over the FULL range; over tau>=0 it
   survives and swamps the answer (0.5473 against the true -0.0949).

   WHY: twirl_cal measures a single-qubit observable in a basis that
   OVERLAPS its probe, so <O> is nonzero at t=0. The paper's readout is
   CROSS-Pauli precisely to avoid this - prepare in basis a, measure in
   basis b, keep only pairs with |A xor B| ODD. Then <P_B> = 0 in an
   a-basis eigenstate, F(0) = 0, and the even part is killed at source.

   MEASURED, X-basis product eigenstate, various observables:
       IIY   F(0)=0.000000   F(t)+F(-t)=+0.000000   ODD
       YYY   F(0)=0.000000   F(t)+F(-t)=+0.000000   ODD
       IZY   F(0)=0.000000   F(t)+F(-t)=+0.000000   ODD
       IIX   F(0)=1.000000   F(t)+F(-t)=+1.921509   not odd (same basis)
       IYY   F(0)=0.000000   F(t)+F(-t)=+0.099727   not odd

   SO THE VISIBILITY RULE IS NOT BOOKKEEPING. The odd-|A xor B| condition
   in their Algorithm 2, which reads like an indexing detail, is what
   makes the protocol runnable WITHOUT TIME REVERSAL on an always-on
   device. Drop it and the kernel demands negative evolution times.

   CONSEQUENCE FOR THE BUILD: swapping fixed-T for the kernel is not a
   patch. It requires restructuring the readout to cross-Pauli with the
   cyclic (c,a,b) rule and the q(u) visibility reweighting - i.e.
   implementing Algorithm 2 with the register in front of it, which is
   the whole job rather than a substitution. Q1 and Q2 stay OPEN.

